  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 1111 PM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nThe aforementioned strong ridge to slide east into the Ohio River \nValley Wednesday, allowing a fast moving shortwave and associated \nsurface low to lift northeast from the Great Plains into the Upper \nMidwest. This will drag a cool front through the CWA on Thursday. \nAhead of the front, strong return flow evident by 850mb winds \nincreasing around 50kts, to allow for breezy south winds to advect \nin warmer temps. Well above normal temperatures in the upper \n50s/lower 60s Tuesday through Saturday are forecast! With the NBM \ndewpoints verifying too high as of late, we may need to watch for \nany fire wx concerns given our dry antecedent conditions and gusty \nsouthwest winds Wednesday/Thursday. One thing to note, is that most \nof the fire concerns would be for standing / unharvested crops, \nwhich is decreasing rapidly in coverage, and may soon be finished.\n\nRegarding precipitation chances, ensemble forecast soundings and \nlayer RH progs suggest there will be plenty of dry air to overcome \nresulting in mainly sprinkles/virga Wednesday night aside from what \nthe NBM shows (10-15% PoPs and zero QPF). These PoPs may end up \nbeing completely removed in later forecasts. The western CONUS upper \nlow to then eject a shortwave into the Plains, resulting in 15-60% \nPoPs Friday afternoon through Saturday. Taking a look at a dprog/dt \nof the NBM QPF, there is a downward trend in >0.1\" probabilities \nending 12z Sat with highest values (40-50%) northwest of Cedar \nRapids. There remains considerable differences with the evolution of \nthis wave in the deterministic and ensemble runs of the GFS/ECMWF. \nSomething to watch will be whether there will be any phasing of a \nnorthern stream wave Saturday as the 00z ECMWF suggests, which could \nbring a slower system and higher PoPs along a cold front.",
